-- title:
--   Definitions for Smooth Functors vs. Differential Forms
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Smooth Functors vs. Differential Forms* (arXiv:0802.0663) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `IsSittingPath`: Section 4.1, PDF p. 50, and sitting instants on pp. 6–7. A sitting path is represented by its constant extension to the real line. Sitting at both ends makes this equivalent to the closed-interval convention. The existential sitting instant belongs to this path alone, not to a family.
--   - `sittingPathConcat`: Section 4.1, PDF p. 50: the usual half-speed concatenation, with gammaTwo traversed after gammaOne. The accepted same-short-name Information100.P69.concatenate tensors quantum channels and has a different type.
--   - `SittingBigon`: Definition 2.1, PDF p. 15: a smooth bigon, represented by its constant sitting extension to the plane. The same epsilon works on the whole square. No epsilon is required to work for a family of bigons.
--   - `constantBigon`: The constant identity bigon, Definition 2.3, PDF p. 16.
--   - `manifoldDiffeology`: Section 1.2, PDF p. 9: canonical manifold diffeology generated by smooth maps from Euclidean spaces. Locality supplies the open-domain plots; this uses Mathlib's full-Euclidean-domain convention for diffeology.
--   - `bigonQuotientDiffeology`: Section 1.2 and Lemma 2.4, PDF pp. 9,17: the quotient diffeology for the named projection p. Its generators are the images of jointly smooth families of sitting bigons. Diffeological generation includes locality, so plots may lift only locally. No global lift or family-wide sitting instant is required. The surjectivity of p is imposed at the theorem.
--   - `CrossedModuleEquations`: Definition A.7, PDF p. 66: the homomorphism, automorphic left action, and both Peiffer identities. Smoothness is added separately below.
--   - `crossedCoordinateSource`: Source of the actual arrow (g,h), Appendix A.2, PDF p. 66. Renew46.P18.crossedSource instead takes a bundled action and an H-first SemidirectProduct; this map is on the raw G-first product manifold.
--   - `crossedCoordinateTarget`: Target of the actual arrow (g,h), equation (A.5), PDF p. 67. The raw map t is not yet bundled as a homomorphism; the crossed-module hypothesis supplies its laws in the construction theorem.
--   - `crossedCoordinateIdentity`: Identity arrow at g, Appendix A.2, PDF p. 66.
--   - `crossedVertical`: Vertical composition b after a, equation (A.4), PDF p. 66. Its categorical domain is crossedCoordinateSource b = crossedCoordinateTarget t a; the displayed formula also gives a smooth extension off that domain.
--   - `crossedVerticalInverse`: Groupoid inverse, separate from tensor inverse, PDF pp. 66–67.
--   - `crossedTensor`: The tensor/semidirect product in the source's G-first coordinates, equation (A.3), PDF p. 66.
--   - `crossedTensorInverse`: The inverse functor on arrows, Appendix A.2, PDF p. 67.
--   - `IsSmoothCrossedModule`: Definition A.7, PDF p. 66: smooth crossed-module data on the specified Lie groups. Its laws are not consequences assumed of the strict Lie 2-group construction.
--   - `IsConstructedStrictLieTwoGroup`: Definitions A.6–A.7, PDF pp. 65–67, spelled out in the computed G-first arrow coordinates. Object tensor and inverse are the given group operations on G. The source and target are surjective submersions; the identity is a smooth embedding (including injective tangent maps). All arrow operations are smooth. Vertical composition is only asserted on matching arrows, while its extension is smooth on the ambient product. The remaining clauses give the full groupoid, strict tensor, interchange, and inverse-functor laws, including the strict inverse equations (A.2).
-- source:
--   Smooth Functors vs. Differential Forms, arXiv:0802.0663v4, https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.0663v4

import Mathlib

/-!
Statements from Schreiber–Waldorf, arXiv:0802.0663v4.
New definitions are local to this paper. The remaining nodes and their precise
missing interfaces are inventoried in HARD.md and GAPS.json.
Compilation and independent moderation are pending batch handoff.
-/

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Intake100.P15

open scoped Manifold ContDiff

universe u v w z

/-- Section 4.1, PDF p. 50, and sitting instants on pp. 6–7.
A sitting path is represented by its constant extension to the real line.
Sitting at both ends makes this equivalent to the closed-interval convention.
The existential sitting instant belongs to this path alone, not to a family. -/
def IsSittingPath {X : Type u} [DiffeologicalSpace X] (gamma : ℝ → X) : Prop :=
  Diffeology.DSmooth gamma ∧
    ∃ eps : ℝ, 0 < eps ∧ eps < 1 / 2 ∧
      (∀ t : ℝ, t < eps → gamma t = gamma 0) ∧
      (∀ t : ℝ, 1 - eps < t → gamma t = gamma 1)

/-- Section 4.1, PDF p. 50: the usual half-speed concatenation,
with gammaTwo traversed after gammaOne. The accepted same-short-name
Information100.P69.concatenate tensors quantum channels and has a different type. -/
noncomputable def sittingPathConcat {X : Type u} (gammaTwo gammaOne : ℝ → X) : ℝ → X :=
  fun t => if t < 1 / 2 then gammaOne (2 * t) else gammaTwo (2 * t - 1)

/-- Sanity: every point supplies a constant sitting path. -/
example {X : Type u} [DiffeologicalSpace X] (x : X) :
    IsSittingPath (fun _ : ℝ => x) := by
  refine ⟨Diffeology.dSmooth_const, 1 / 4, by norm_num, by norm_num, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro t ht
    rfl
  · intro t ht
    rfl

/-- Sanity: the joining parameter uses the source of the second path. -/
example {X : Type u} (gammaTwo gammaOne : ℝ → X) :
    sittingPathConcat gammaTwo gammaOne (1 / 2) = gammaTwo 0 := by
  norm_num [sittingPathConcat]

section BigonPlots

variable {E : Type w} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
variable {X : Type u} [TopologicalSpace X] [ChartedSpace E X]

/-- Definition 2.1, PDF p. 15: a smooth bigon, represented by its
constant sitting extension to the plane. The same epsilon works on the
whole square. No epsilon is required to work for a family of bigons. -/
structure SittingBigon (E : Type w) [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    (X : Type u) [TopologicalSpace X] [ChartedSpace E X] where
  map : ℝ × ℝ → X
  smooth : ContMDiff ((𝓘(ℝ, ℝ)).prod (𝓘(ℝ, ℝ))) (𝓘(ℝ, E)) ∞ map
  sitting : ∃ eps : ℝ, 0 < eps ∧ eps < 1 / 2 ∧
    (∀ s t : ℝ, t < eps → map (s, t) = map (0, 0)) ∧
    (∀ s t : ℝ, 1 - eps < t → map (s, t) = map (0, 1)) ∧
    (∀ s t : ℝ, s < eps → map (s, t) = map (0, t)) ∧
    (∀ s t : ℝ, 1 - eps < s → map (s, t) = map (1, t))

/-- The constant identity bigon, Definition 2.3, PDF p. 16. -/
def constantBigon (x : X) : SittingBigon E X where
  map := fun _ => x
  smooth := contMDiff_const
  sitting := by
    refine ⟨1 / 4, by norm_num, by norm_num, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩ <;>
      intro s t ht <;> rfl

/-- Sanity: both boundary paths of a constant bigon have the same value. -/
example (x : X) (t : ℝ) :
    (constantBigon (E := E) x).map (0, t) = x ∧
    (constantBigon (E := E) x).map (1, t) = x := ⟨rfl, rfl⟩

/-- Section 1.2, PDF p. 9: canonical manifold diffeology generated by
smooth maps from Euclidean spaces. Locality supplies the open-domain plots;
this uses Mathlib's full-Euclidean-domain convention for diffeology. -/
noncomputable def manifoldDiffeology : DiffeologicalSpace X :=
  DiffeologicalSpace.generateFrom
    {p | ContMDiff (𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p.1))) (𝓘(ℝ, E)) ∞ p.2}

/-- Section 1.2 and Lemma 2.4, PDF pp. 9,17: the quotient diffeology
for the named projection p. Its generators are the images of jointly smooth
families of sitting bigons. Diffeological generation includes locality, so
plots may lift only locally. No global lift or family-wide sitting instant
is required. The surjectivity of p is imposed at the theorem. -/
noncomputable def bigonQuotientDiffeology {Q : Type v}
    (p : SittingBigon E X → Q) : DiffeologicalSpace Q :=
  DiffeologicalSpace.generateFrom
    {q | ∃ c : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin q.1) → SittingBigon E X,
      ContMDiff
        ((𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin q.1))).prod
          ((𝓘(ℝ, ℝ)).prod (𝓘(ℝ, ℝ))))
        (𝓘(ℝ, E)) ∞ (fun z : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin q.1) × (ℝ × ℝ) => (c z.1).map z.2) ∧
      q.2 = p ∘ c}

/-- Sanity: constant maps are generating plots of the manifold diffeology. -/
example (x : X) (n : ℕ) :
    @Diffeology.IsPlot X (manifoldDiffeology (E := E)) n
      (fun _ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) => x) := by
  apply DiffeologicalSpace.isPlot_generatedFrom_of_mem
  exact contMDiff_const

/-- Sanity: a constant bigon family gives a quotient plot even when
its projection is not injective. -/
example {Q : Type v} (p : SittingBigon E X → Q) (x : X) (n : ℕ) :
    @Diffeology.IsPlot Q (bigonQuotientDiffeology p) n
      (p ∘ (fun _ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) => constantBigon (E := E) x)) := by
  unfold bigonQuotientDiffeology
  apply DiffeologicalSpace.isPlot_generatedFrom_of_mem
  exact ⟨(fun _ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) => constantBigon (E := E) x),
    contMDiff_const, rfl⟩

end BigonPlots

section CrossedModuleCoordinates

variable {G : Type u} {H : Type v} [Group G] [Group H]

/-- Definition A.7, PDF p. 66: the homomorphism, automorphic left action,
and both Peiffer identities. Smoothness is added separately below. -/
def CrossedModuleEquations (t : H → G) (alpha : G × H → H) : Prop :=
  t 1 = 1 ∧
  (∀ h k, t (h * k) = t h * t k) ∧
  (∀ g, alpha (g, 1) = 1) ∧
  (∀ g h k, alpha (g, h * k) = alpha (g, h) * alpha (g, k)) ∧
  (∀ h, alpha (1, h) = h) ∧
  (∀ g k h, alpha (g * k, h) = alpha (g, alpha (k, h))) ∧
  (∀ g h, t (alpha (g, h)) = g * t h * g⁻¹) ∧
  (∀ h k, alpha (t h, k) = h * k * h⁻¹)

/-- Source of the actual arrow (g,h), Appendix A.2, PDF p. 66.
Renew46.P18.crossedSource instead takes a bundled action and an H-first
SemidirectProduct; this map is on the raw G-first product manifold. -/
def crossedCoordinateSource (a : G × H) : G := a.1

/-- Target of the actual arrow (g,h), equation (A.5), PDF p. 67.
The raw map t is not yet bundled as a homomorphism; the crossed-module
hypothesis supplies its laws in the construction theorem. -/
def crossedCoordinateTarget (t : H → G) (a : G × H) : G := t a.2 * a.1

/-- Identity arrow at g, Appendix A.2, PDF p. 66. -/
def crossedCoordinateIdentity (g : G) : G × H := (g, 1)

/-- Vertical composition b after a, equation (A.4), PDF p. 66.
Its categorical domain is crossedCoordinateSource b = crossedCoordinateTarget t a;
the displayed formula also gives a smooth extension off that domain. -/
def crossedVertical (b a : G × H) : G × H := (a.1, b.2 * a.2)

/-- Groupoid inverse, separate from tensor inverse, PDF pp. 66–67. -/
def crossedVerticalInverse (t : H → G) (a : G × H) : G × H :=
  (t a.2 * a.1, a.2⁻¹)

/-- The tensor/semidirect product in the source's G-first coordinates,
equation (A.3), PDF p. 66. -/
def crossedTensor (alpha : G × H → H) (b a : G × H) : G × H :=
  (b.1 * a.1, b.2 * alpha (b.1, a.2))

/-- The inverse functor on arrows, Appendix A.2, PDF p. 67. -/
def crossedTensorInverse (alpha : G × H → H) (a : G × H) : G × H :=
  (a.1⁻¹, alpha (a.1⁻¹, a.2⁻¹))

/-- Sanity: vertical composition has the source of the first arrow
and multiplies H labels in traversal order. -/
example (b a : G × H) :
    crossedCoordinateSource (crossedVertical b a) = a.1 ∧
    (crossedVertical b a).2 = b.2 * a.2 := ⟨rfl, rfl⟩

/-- Sanity: horizontal composition acts by the source G-label of b. -/
example (alpha : G × H → H) (b a : G × H) :
    (crossedTensor alpha b a).2 = b.2 * alpha (b.1, a.2) := rfl

/-- Sanity: the categorical inverse swaps source and target coordinates. -/
example (t : H → G) (a : G × H) :
    crossedCoordinateSource (crossedVerticalInverse t a) = crossedCoordinateTarget t a := rfl

/-- Sanity: the tensor inverse uses the inverse G label to act. -/
example (alpha : G × H → H) (a : G × H) :
    (crossedTensorInverse alpha a).1 = a.1⁻¹ ∧
    (crossedTensorInverse alpha a).2 = alpha (a.1⁻¹, a.2⁻¹) := ⟨rfl, rfl⟩

end CrossedModuleCoordinates

section SmoothCrossedModules

variable {E : Type w} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
variable {F : Type z} [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F]
variable {G : Type u} [Group G] [TopologicalSpace G] [ChartedSpace E G]
variable {H : Type v} [Group H] [TopologicalSpace H] [ChartedSpace F H]

/-- Definition A.7, PDF p. 66: smooth crossed-module data on the
specified Lie groups. Its laws are not consequences assumed of the
strict Lie 2-group construction. -/
def IsSmoothCrossedModule (t : H → G) (alpha : G × H → H) : Prop :=
  ContMDiff (𝓘(ℝ, F)) (𝓘(ℝ, E)) ∞ t ∧
  ContMDiff ((𝓘(ℝ, E)).prod (𝓘(ℝ, F))) (𝓘(ℝ, F)) ∞ alpha ∧
  CrossedModuleEquations t alpha

/-- Definitions A.6–A.7, PDF pp. 65–67, spelled out in the computed
G-first arrow coordinates. Object tensor and inverse are the given group
operations on G. The source and target are surjective submersions; the
identity is a smooth embedding (including injective tangent maps).
All arrow operations are smooth. Vertical composition is only asserted
on matching arrows, while its extension is smooth on the ambient product.
The remaining clauses give the full groupoid, strict tensor, interchange,
and inverse-functor laws, including the strict inverse equations (A.2). -/
def IsConstructedStrictLieTwoGroup (t : H → G) (alpha : G × H → H) : Prop :=
  let IG := 𝓘(ℝ, E)
  let IH := 𝓘(ℝ, F)
  let IA := IG.prod IH
  let s : G × H → G := crossedCoordinateSource
  let target : G × H → G := crossedCoordinateTarget t
  let identity : G → G × H := crossedCoordinateIdentity
  let vertical : (G × H) → (G × H) → (G × H) := crossedVertical
  let verticalInv : G × H → G × H := crossedVerticalInverse t
  let tensor : (G × H) → (G × H) → (G × H) := crossedTensor alpha
  let tensorInv : G × H → G × H := crossedTensorInverse alpha
  let unit : G × H := (1, 1)
  ContMDiff (IG.prod IG) IG ∞ (fun p : G × G => p.1 * p.2) ∧
  ContMDiff IG IG ∞ (fun g : G => g⁻¹) ∧
  ContMDiff IA IG ∞ s ∧
  ContMDiff IA IG ∞ target ∧
  Function.Surjective s ∧ Function.Surjective target ∧
  (∀ a, Function.Surjective (mfderiv IA IG s a)) ∧
  (∀ a, Function.Surjective (mfderiv IA IG target a)) ∧
  ContMDiff IG IA ∞ identity ∧ Topology.IsEmbedding identity ∧
  (∀ g, Function.Injective (mfderiv IG IA identity g)) ∧
  ContMDiff (IA.prod IA) IA ∞ (fun p : (G × H) × (G × H) => vertical p.1 p.2) ∧
  ContMDiff IA IA ∞ verticalInv ∧
  ContMDiff (IA.prod IA) IA ∞ (fun p : (G × H) × (G × H) => tensor p.1 p.2) ∧
  ContMDiff IA IA ∞ tensorInv ∧
  (∀ g, s (identity g) = g ∧ target (identity g) = g) ∧
  (∀ b a, s b = target a →
    s (vertical b a) = s a ∧ target (vertical b a) = target b) ∧
  (∀ a, vertical (identity (target a)) a = a ∧
    vertical a (identity (s a)) = a) ∧
  (∀ c b a, s b = target a → s c = target b →
    vertical c (vertical b a) = vertical (vertical c b) a) ∧
  (∀ a, s (verticalInv a) = target a ∧ target (verticalInv a) = s a ∧
    vertical (verticalInv a) a = identity (s a) ∧
    vertical a (verticalInv a) = identity (target a)) ∧
  (∀ b a, s (tensor b a) = s b * s a ∧
    target (tensor b a) = target b * target a) ∧
  (∀ b a, tensor (identity b) (identity a) = identity (b * a)) ∧
  (∀ d c b a, s d = target c → s b = target a →
    vertical (tensor d b) (tensor c a) = tensor (vertical d c) (vertical b a)) ∧
  (∀ c b a, tensor c (tensor b a) = tensor (tensor c b) a) ∧
  (∀ a, tensor unit a = a ∧ tensor a unit = a) ∧
  (∀ a, s (tensorInv a) = (s a)⁻¹ ∧ target (tensorInv a) = (target a)⁻¹) ∧
  (∀ g, tensorInv (identity g) = identity g⁻¹) ∧
  (∀ b a, s b = target a →
    tensorInv (vertical b a) = vertical (tensorInv b) (tensorInv a)) ∧
  (∀ a, tensor a (tensorInv a) = unit ∧ tensor (tensorInv a) a = unit)

/-- Sanity: the identity crossed module uses actual smooth maps.
This also checks consistency of the action and both Peiffer identities. -/
example [LieGroup (𝓘(ℝ, E)) ∞ G] :
    IsSmoothCrossedModule (E := E) (F := E)
      (id : G → G) (fun p : G × G => p.1 * p.2 * p.1⁻¹) := by
  refine ⟨contMDiff_id, ?_, ?_⟩
  · exact (contMDiff_fst.mul contMDiff_snd).mul contMDiff_fst.inv
  · simp [CrossedModuleEquations, mul_assoc]

end SmoothCrossedModules

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Intake100.P15


